<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Coimbra</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Aeminium</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Coimbra is a city in central Portugal on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mondego">Mondego</a> river. Roman Aeminium, it was taken from the Muslims in 878, lost to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a> in 987 and finally reconquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-leon">Ferdinand I of León</a> in 1064. It served as capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal</a> from 1131 to 1255, and its university, founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> in 1290 and settled at Coimbra permanently in 1537, is one of the oldest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/coimbra.jpg" alt="Coimbra" width="320" height="162" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Leandro Neumann Ciuffo · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coimbra_e_o_rio_Mondego_(6167200429)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
